{"id": "938e381f-8f1c-4aa3-8afe-139f4c0c6bcb", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "Not necessarily — `0x80072F8F` is very commonly a **clock/time problem**, not a certificate trust problem, and the symptom profile you're describing (stopped for two months, then powered on) is the classic trigger for exactly that.

Here's why: when an EC2 instance is stopped, its hardware clock stops updating, and the time sync service (`w32time`) may not catch up quickly or at all on resume, especially if it was configured to sync infrequently. On boot, the clock can be off by days, weeks, or (in your case) up to two months. Since TLS certificate validation checks the chain against the system clock, a sufficiently stale clock makes otherwise-valid Windows Update/Microsoft Update certs look "not yet valid" or "expired" — Windows surfaces that as `0x80072F8F` ("A security error occurred").

